<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Chiefdom of Nicoya</h1><p class="st-meta">800 CE – 1524 CE (approx.) · capital Nicoya</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The leading chiefdom of the Chorotega people on the Nicoya peninsula of Costa Rica, met by Spanish explorers in 1522-23.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chorotega">Chorotega</a>, speakers of an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oto-manguean-languages">Oto-Manguean</a> language, are thought to have migrated from central <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mexico">Mexico</a> to the Pacific coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a> around 800. They settled the Nicoya peninsula and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-nicoya">Gulf of Nicoya</a> area, where several chiefdoms emerged.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Nicoya was ruled by a chief who bore the same name as his territory. Society was divided into nobles, commoners and slaves, and councils of elders advised the chief. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chorotega">Chorotega</a> produced fine polychrome pottery and carved jade and stone metates, grew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacao">cacao</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, and used a ritual calendar of Mesoamerican type. They also made purple dye from shellfish.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Spanish expedition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gil-gonzalez-davila">Gil González Dávila</a> reached Nicoya in 1522-23, and the chief accepted baptism. Colonial rule, slaving and disease devastated the population. Nicoya became an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encomienda">encomienda</a> and later part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-costa-rica">Costa Rica</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 800 CE – 1524 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">chiefdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:75.62%;width:14.41%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>800 CE – 1524 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nicoya</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Nicoya</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Chorotega religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Chorotega</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/costa-rica">Costa Rica</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Nicoya, Chorotega chiefdom of Nicoya</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1162&amp;focus=chiefdom-of-nicoya" aria-label="Chiefdom of Nicoya on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2323 1800 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Chiefdom of Nicoya lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2643 2056L4910 989"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4910 989h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2643 2056h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2643 2056h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Nicoya. <a href="/atlas?year=1162&amp;focus=chiefdom-of-nicoya">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

